      "findingDispositions": [
        {
          "findingId": "B-3e16:F-014",
          "disposition": "verified_family",
          "familyKey": "concept-completeness-unwired-CHK-003-acceptance-guards",
          "reason": "Independently confirmed cli-surface-smoke and verify-orphan-surface are absent from proof-or-stop.config.json validate.commands and root package.json test:serial-reference (same grep result as F-012), and independently confirmed checklist.json CHK-003 userSees names exactly these two plus command-ownership-contract and command-catalog-smoke as acceptance evidence. The distinguishing claim beyond F-012 -- that this story's OWN acceptance criteria cites unwired guards as evidence, making it a concept-completeness defect in this story's deliverable rather than a general doc-accuracy complaint -- is independently verified and correctly scoped as a separate, non-duplicate family from F-012 (see F-012 reasoning for why they do not auto-cluster)."
        },
        {
          "findingId": "B-3e16:F-015",
          "disposition": "verified_family",
          "familyKey": "doc-missing-binding-reason-for-dynamic-import",
          "reason": "Independently read packages/graph/README.md and the graph.ts MODULE RESOLUTION header: both currently give only the removable reason (no workspaces / not a declared dependency) for the dynamic import, and neither states the binding reason (node:sqlite is unavoidably reachable through any static import chain into the graph library, per F-010's independently-confirmed empirical finding on Node v20.19.6). Confirmed the decisions doc frames itself as recording exactly this class of non-obvious rationale. This finding's recommendation is now more urgent, not less, given the plan explicitly keeps the dynamic import permanently rather than as a temporary stopgap -- if the binding reason is never written down, a future contributor who adds packages/graph as a dependency has no documented reason not to also convert the import to static, silently reintroducing the F-010 regression. Concurs with docs lane."
        },
        {
          "findingId": "B-3e16:F-016",
          "disposition": "verified_family",
          "familyKey": "guard-narrowness-no-mechanical-reverification",
          "reason": "Independently read isKnownBenignNonLiteralLoaderCall in retired-authority-vocabulary-guard-smoke.ts: it checks only (a) the whole-file AST fingerprint matches the pinned digest and (b) the enclosing function name plus the call's first-argument TEXT matches an approved {anchor,argument} pair. It performs no check on the argument's underlying data-flow provenance (e.g., that location.entry still traces only to resolveGraphPackage()/packageRoot rather than becoming caller-influenced) -- confirmed by reading the full function body and its call sites at lines ~1537-1548. The mechanism proves 'the file has not changed since approval', not 'the approved narrow shape still holds after a semantically-different edit that preserves the same textual anchor/argument shape'. This is a real, currently-unaddressed mechanical gap that would also apply, with equal force, to the planned graph-runtime.ts extraction itself: extracting the loader into a smaller file does not add a provenance check, it only shrinks the blast radius of what a whole-file fingerprint pins -- the underlying reliance on human-written re-approval-basis comments (rather than a mechanical assertion) is unchanged by the extraction. Concurs with test-coverage lane and extends its relevance to the planned remediation."
        },
        {
          "findingId": "B-3e16:F-017",
          "disposition": "verified_family",
          "familyKey": "security-caller-controlled-root-dynamic-import-precedent",
          "reason": "Independently traced the same chain: cli-engine.ts strips a global --root flag into rootOverride, cli/runtime-root.ts resolveRoot returns it verbatim with no sanitization for any command other than story_discard, and ctx.root reaches resolveGraphPackage(ctx.root) in graph.ts. Independently confirmed the existing precedent this finding cites -- verifier/serve.ts startCircuitServe is already in the same knownBenignNonLiteralLoaderCalls allowlist and walks a --root-derived directory to dynamically import whatever machine.ts it finds -- so the trust model genuinely already exists and graph.ts extends rather than introduces it. Independently confirmed the dual-map maintenance cost claim: knownBenignNonLiteralLoaderCalls/BENIGN_NON_LITERAL_LOADER_SOURCE_FINGERPRINTS and DYNAMIC_CODE_GENERATION_FINGERPRINT_EXEMPTIONS are two separate maps in the same file, both pinning whole-file digests, so a single future edit to graph.ts (or to a would-be graph-runtime.ts) needs two coordinated re-pins -- consistent with the existing bin/bootstrap-runner.js entries in both maps, which is real precedent for this being an ongoing, not one-time, maintenance cost. Concurs with quality lane; this is the wave's strongest finding because it is the only one that surfaced a security property (attacker-controlled --root reaching a live dynamic import with zero markers required) that none of the other three lanes, nor the wave-1 critic, examined."
        }
